{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review the explicit back-pressure implementation at exact clean head f116097aacda9aee74ccdac0d6d762f9fe900f2f, on branch request/kernel-backpressure, pushed to origin. Rest your verdict on artifact fda8bc9b and name it — there is one artifact at this head, so there is no ambiguity to resolve this time.\n\nThis is part two of request c8b1215d, the kernel admission gaps. What it implements is the design's own sentence: the sequencer has five named refusals, one of which is back-pressure, and at capacity it should refuse before chaining, cheaply, and say so. Neither half existed. Submitter.Submit queued every caller on its mutex with no bound, and the only overload outcome was an exhausted CAS retry limit returned as a bare error indistinguishable from any other failure.\n\nMaxQueueDepth bounds the submissions inside Submit at once; over the bound Submit returns ErrBackPressure after one atomic add taken before the lock. Zero leaves the queue unbounded, which is what every deployment has today, so nothing changes for anyone who does not set it. Both exhausted-retry sites wrap the same sentinel so callers can use errors.Is.\n\nFull gates at the exact head: vet 0, build 0, test 0, nineteen packages, no failures.\n\nFour things I would particularly like you to push on.\n\nThe Rotate change. I made the exhausted retry budget in Rotate report back-pressure too, on the argument that it is the same contention against the same ref. That is a judgement call about whether \"sequencer at capacity\" is an honest description of a rotation that could not chain, and you may think it overreaches. Rotate has no failpoint in its loop, so its test reaches the line with a negative retry budget rather than by real contention — a weaker proof than the submit-path test, and I would rather you judge that than have me defend it.\n\nThe scope I declined. MaxQueueDepth is not in docs/reference/limits.md, because no flag or configuration reaches it, exactly like the existing undocumented MaxRetries. I also set no default in the resident. Both are deliberate and both are arguable; if you think a limit that exists should be documented even when unreachable, say so and I will add it.\n\nWhether the bound is the right shape. It counts submissions inside Submit, which on the resident path is the mutex queue. The stateless Submit function has no shared state and so has no bound at all — I concluded a queue depth is meaningless there, but that does leave one of the two entry points unprotected.\n\nThe tests, adversarially. They pass, which proves little on its own, so I mutated the implementation: deleting the gate fails; moving the gate after the lock, where it still refuses but only after queuing, also fails; reverting either exhaustion site to an anonymous error fails. The middle one is the load-bearing check, because it is what distinguishes \"before chaining\" from \"returns an error\". If you can think of a mutation that survives, that is the review I want.\n\nOne thing to know before you merge. The first candidate I published, cb469e42, was stale from birth and I have retired it naming this replacement. The cause is not this work: request c8b1215d rests on assert 95e79d71, which rests on artifact a46bc9df, which rests on the long-retired artifact 60fbb639, so the request was already stale when I promised on it and anything resting on that promise inherits it. This artifact rests instead on d97eed89, the live non-stale predecessor at this same path. The commit still carries Rests-On naming c8b1215d, so the governing link is intact.\n\nAlso for the merge step: internal/kernel currently carries a third live artifact, your 9999be33 at 63d8d765, which is stale and predates d97eed89. It is one of the twenty-one in beefb8f6 and only you can retire it. Clearing it would leave this path with one live artifact before merge rather than after.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fda8bc9b261e96583d8e99d86e722ed8978389cd","conditions":"An independent exact-head review of f116097aacda9aee74ccdac0d6d762f9fe900f2f resting on and naming artifact fda8bc9b: the full gates pass at that head; the back-pressure refusal is returned before any chaining work and is distinguishable via errors.Is; the tests pin the behaviour rather than passing decoratively; the Rotate exhaustion site is judged either correct or over-reaching and said so explicitly; the architecture layering and security boundaries are stated; and any concrete simplification is identified. Report approved or changes-requested with body head, artifact and verdict.","head":"f116097aacda9aee74ccdac0d6d762f9fe900f2f","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}